    tags: [
      "how much to spend on a treadmill",
      "treadmill budget",
      "treadmill buying guide",
      "treadmill value",
      "home gym budget",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See the Framework", link: "#question-one" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-howmuchshouldyouspendonatreadmill" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-howmuchshouldyouspendonatreadmill)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          How Much Should You <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Spend on a Treadmill?</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Not a price list — a decision framework. Five questions about your actual use determine the number, and for most people it lands lower than the machine they were about to buy.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#question-one" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Start the Five Questions
          </a>
          <a href="#profiles" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Budget by Profile
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 8, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">One number to have ready.</strong> Before starting, decide
          how many sessions a week you will realistically do in month six, not in week one. Every
          calculation on this page depends on that figure, and being honest about it is the single
          most valuable thing you can do for this decision.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#question-one" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Question One: Will You Walk or Run?</a>
          <a href="#question-two" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Question Two: How Often, and For How Many Years?</a>
          <a href="#question-three" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Question Three: Who Else Will Use It?</a>
          <a href="#question-four" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Question Four: Your Height and Weight</a>
          <a href="#question-five" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Question Five: What Room, and Will It Move?</a>
          <a href="#profiles" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Budget by Profile</a>
          <a href="#cost-per-use" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. The Cost-Per-Use Arithmetic</a>
          <a href="#subscription-cost" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Count the Subscription Before You Compare Anything</a>
          <a href="#last-200" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Where to Spend the Last $200</a>
          <a href="#spend-nothing" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. When the Answer Is to Spend Nothing</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Final Recommendations</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost every treadmill budget is set the wrong way round — a number is chosen first and
          a machine found to fit it. Set the use first and the budget falls out of it, and the
          result is usually less money spent on a machine that suits you better.
        </p>

        <div id="question-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Question One: Will You Walk or Run?</h2>
<p>
            This determines more of your budget than the other four questions combined, because it
            sets the deck length, and deck length is the specification that no other component
            compensates for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you will walk</strong>, including incline walking, a 50 to 55-inch deck is
            ample and your budget floor is around $500. Everything above roughly $1,000 buys
            durability and comfort rather than capability, and a great deal of it will go unused.
            Most treadmills are used at 2.5 to 4 mph regardless of price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you will run</strong>, you need a 60-inch deck if you are over about 5'8",
            and a frame heavy enough to stay still at pace. That is a floor of roughly $1,200 new,
            or $800 used. Below it you will shorten your stride to stay clear of the rear roller,
            which is how treadmill running earns its reputation for feeling wrong.
          </p>
          <p>
            Answer this honestly rather than aspirationally. Buying a running machine for a
            walking habit is the most common and most expensive error in this category. Our
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking guide</a>
            and <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running guide</a>
            cover each case.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="question-two" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Question Two: How Often, and For How Many Years?</h2>
<p>
            Frequency determines how much durability margin you need to buy, and the thresholds are
            reasonably clear.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One to two sessions a week</strong> is roughly 100 hours a year. Any machine at
            any price handles that, and durability arguments for spending more largely evaporate.
            Buy for comfort and features instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Three to four sessions a week</strong> is 150 to 250 hours a year — the typical
            committed home user. A mid-range machine handles this for eight to ten years with
            maintenance. This is the profile most of the market is designed around.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Five or more sessions a week</strong>, particularly running, is 300+ hours a
            year and genuinely heavy home use. Here the durability argument becomes real, and the
            components that separate mid-range from premium — motor type, roller diameter — start
            to matter within your ownership window rather than after it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then multiply by years. If you expect to move house or upgrade in three years, buying
            fifteen-year hardware is buying a benefit you will sell to someone else at a loss.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="question-three" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Question Three: Who Else Will Use It?</h2>
<p>
            A second regular user does not double the wear in a simple sense, but it does move you
            up a tier, and it is the most commonly forgotten input.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two people at three sessions each is 300 to 400 hours a year, which puts a mid-range DC
            motor into genuinely heavy use. Three or more regular users, or two people running
            rather than walking, is the point at which a commercial AC motor stops being
            aspirational and becomes the sensible engineering choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            Multiple users also changes which specifications matter. If one user is much taller,
            buy for their stride — a long deck costs a shorter user nothing. If one user has
            balance or mobility limitations, step-up height and handrail length become as important
            as the motor, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide for older walkers</a>
            covers what to look for. If users differ greatly in height, a tilting console is worth
            more than it appears.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="question-four" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Question Four: Your Height and Weight</h2>
<p>
            Both set hard floors that no amount of shopping negotiates around.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Height.</strong> Under 5'8", a 55-inch deck works for everything including
            light running. Between 5'8" and 6 feet, 60 inches is strongly preferable if you run.
            Above 6 feet, 60 inches is the minimum, and above 6'4" a 22-inch belt width becomes
            worth paying for as well. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tall runners guide</a>
            covers the measurements.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight.</strong> Shop 50 to 75 lbs above your actual weight, because the stated
            capacity is a warranty boundary rather than a comfortable operating point. At 200 lbs
            most mid-range machines are comfortable. At 250 lbs you are near the practical ceiling
            of machines rated 300 lbs, and should be looking at 350 lb-plus ratings, which
            generally means spending more. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity guide</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            These two are the constraints most likely to raise your floor above what the earlier
            questions suggested, and ignoring them produces a machine that is uncomfortable from
            the first session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="question-five" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Question Five: What Room, and Will It Move?</h2>
<p>
            The room adds requirements that cost money and are easy to discover too late.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Does it need to fold?</strong> If the machine shares a room, you need a fold
            that works with one person in under a minute — which in practice means a hydraulic or
            gas-strut assist, and those appear from around $900. A manual latch on a cheaper
            machine will be folded for about ten days and then left up permanently.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Is anyone below you?</strong> Structure-borne footfall energy is what a
            downstairs neighbour hears, and heavier machines absorb more of it internally. That
            pushes you up rather than down, and a good mat does more than a price tier does. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">apartment guide</a>
            covers it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Can it get in?</strong> Measure the delivery route — doorway widths, stairwell
            turns, landing pivots — before shortlisting. A machine that fits the room and not the
            stairs is the most common delivery failure, and heavier machines are the ones that
            fail. Budget $100 to $300 for room-of-choice delivery on anything over 250 lbs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="profiles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Budget by Profile</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The walker.</strong> Walking or incline walking, three to four times a week,
            one user, average build, needs to fold. <strong>$700-$1,100.</strong> Spend it on frame
            mass and a good fold rather than a screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The desk walker.</strong> Walking while working, most days, no handrails
            needed, must store away. <strong>$300-$600 on a walking pad</strong>, not a treadmill.
            The money goes into belt and motor rather than a frame you would not use.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The recreational runner.</strong> Running three to four times a week, one user,
            under 6 feet. <strong>$1,200-$1,800.</strong> A 60-inch deck on a 250 lb-plus frame,
            and skip the subscription if you can.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The serious runner.</strong> Thirty-plus miles a week, or two runners in the
            house, or over 6'2". <strong>$1,800-$2,800.</strong> Deck width, frame mass and motor
            margin all start to matter within your ownership window.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The high-capacity or multi-user household.</strong> Over 250 lbs, or three-plus
            daily users. <strong>$3,000+.</strong> AC motor and commercial rollers stop being
            luxuries here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The uncertain beginner.</strong> Not sure the habit will hold.
            <strong>$700-$1,000</strong>, or buy used. Enough machine that equipment is not the
            obstacle, little enough that disuse is a disappointment rather than an event.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The Cost-Per-Use Arithmetic</h2>
<p>
            A useful sanity check once you have a number: divide it by the sessions you will
            realistically do.
          </p>
          <p>
            A $1,200 treadmill used three times a week for five years is about 780 sessions, or
            roughly $1.54 each. That compares favourably with almost any gym membership, and it is
            the honest case for buying decent equipment.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same machine used twice a month for two years is 48 sessions at $25 each, which is
            worse than a gym and worse than most classes. Nothing about the machine changed — only
            the usage assumption.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is why the honest frequency answer matters more than any specification. If you
            genuinely do not know, buy at the lower end of your range: the downside of under-buying
            is upgrading in two years with real knowledge, and the downside of over-buying is an
            expensive object you feel guilty about.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscription-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Count the Subscription Before You Compare Anything</h2>
<p>
            The largest single number in most treadmill budgets is not the treadmill. A
            subscription at around $39 a month is roughly $2,350 over five years, which exceeds the
            purchase price of most machines people are choosing between.
          </p>
          <p>
            A $1,300 subscription-free machine costs $1,300 over five years. An $1,800 connected
            machine costs roughly $4,150. Those two look $500 apart and are $2,850 apart, and no
            hardware difference between them accounts for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            That does not make connected machines bad value — for someone who genuinely needs
            external structure, guided content is often the difference between training and not,
            and the right comparison is against a gym membership. But it belongs in the budget from
            the start rather than appearing in month two. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">subscription guide</a>
            covers what each console does unpaid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="last-200" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Where to Spend the Last $200</h2>
<p>
            Once you have a budget, the marginal money has better and worse homes. In descending
            order of value:
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame mass</strong> — the single most perceptible difference between machines,
            and what stops the deck moving under you. <strong>Deck length</strong>, if it takes you
            from 55 to 60 inches and you run; that is a capability threshold rather than an
            improvement. <strong>Roller diameter</strong>, from 1.9 to 2.5 inches, which is the best
            longevity value in the category.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then, further down: <strong>an assisted fold</strong> if the machine shares a room,
            because it determines whether the machine is ever folded; and <strong>a proper
            mat</strong>, which costs under $100 and does more for downstairs noise than any price
            tier.
          </p>
          <p>
            Worth least: screen size, top speed above 10 mph, decline unless you are training for a
            descent, and the difference between 3.0 and 3.5 CHP. All real, none of them things you
            will notice on a given run.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spend-nothing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. When the Answer Is to Spend Nothing</h2>
<p>
            A guide about budgets should be willing to say when the number is zero.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you have a gym membership you use, and the treadmill is intended to replace a
            commute you enjoy, the machine will probably become storage. If you run outdoors
            happily and the treadmill is for perhaps fifteen bad-weather days a year, the
            cost-per-use arithmetic is dismal and a waterproof jacket is a better purchase.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if the honest answer to "will I use this" is "I hope so", the better first step is
            to establish the habit before buying the equipment. Walking outdoors for six weeks
            costs nothing and tells you what you would actually buy — often that a walking pad
            suits you better than a treadmill, which is a $2,000 difference discovered for free.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nobody selling treadmills will tell you this, which is precisely why it belongs on a
            page about how much to spend.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Final Recommendations</h2>
<p>
            For most people the answer is <strong>$900 to $1,500</strong>. That range covers every
            difference you will perceive — frame stability, deck length, a fold that works, a
            warranty in years — and very little that you will not.
          </p>
          <p>
            Spend <strong>less</strong> if you will walk, if you are uncertain, or if you move
            often. A $700 machine you use is worth more than a $2,500 machine you resent.
          </p>
          <p>
            Spend <strong>more</strong> only for a specific reason you can name: multiple daily
            users, over 250 lbs, over 6'2", high mileage, or a fifteen-year horizon in a house you
            are staying in. "It seems better" is not one of those reasons.
          </p>
          <p>
            And whatever the number, reserve $80 of it for a mat and a bottle of lubricant. Those
            two items do more for your experience and the machine's lifespan than the last $300 of
            treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should I spend on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, $900 to $1,500. That range covers every difference you will actually perceive — frame stability, deck length, a fold that works and a warranty in years. Spend less if you will only walk or are unsure the habit will hold; spend more only for a specific reason such as multiple daily users or high body weight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a $500 treadmill worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For walking, yes — most treadmills are used at 2.5 to 4 mph regardless of price, and a budget machine does that competently. For running, no: decks at this price are 40 to 50 inches against the 60 a running stride needs, and frames are light enough to move under you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the minimum I should spend to run on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About $1,200 new, or $800 used. That is the floor for a 60-inch deck on a frame heavy enough to stay still at pace. Below it you shorten your stride to stay clear of the rear roller, which changes your gait and is the main reason treadmill running is said to feel unnatural.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I spend more if two people will use the treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually one tier up. Two people at three sessions each is 300 to 400 hours a year, which puts a mid-range DC motor into genuinely heavy use. Also buy for the taller user's stride — a long deck costs a shorter user nothing — and consider a tilting console if heights differ a lot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I calculate whether a treadmill is worth the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the total cost, subscription included, by the sessions you will realistically do in month six. A $1,200 machine used three times a week for five years is about $1.54 a session, which beats most gym memberships. The same machine used twice a month is $25 a session, which does not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I spend the last $200 of my budget on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Frame mass first, since it is the most perceptible difference between machines. Then deck length if it takes you from 55 to 60 inches and you run, then roller diameter from 1.9 to 2.5 inches. Screen size, top speed and decline are worth least. Reserve about $80 for a mat and lubricant regardless.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Setting a Treadmill Budget</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Three of these are about answering the use question aspirationally rather than honestly, which is the root of most treadmill regret.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for the training you intend to start</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A running machine bought for a walking habit is the most common and most expensive error in this category. Answer the walk-or-run question based on what you have done in the last six months, not what you plan. If you are wrong in the cheap direction, upgrading in two years with real knowledge is a good outcome.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the subscription out of the budget</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">At around $39 a month it is roughly $2,350 over five years — more than most machines being compared. A $1,300 subscription-free treadmill and an $1,800 connected one look $500 apart and are $2,850 apart. Decide about the content before you decide about the hardware, not in month two.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the budget before answering the use questions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Choosing a number and then finding a machine to fit is how people end up with a 55-inch deck they have outgrown or a commercial motor they will never load. Height, weight, frequency and the room all set hard floors. Work out the requirements first and the budget falls out of them.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spending the whole budget on the machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Reserve about $80 for a rubber mat and belt lubricant. The mat does more for downstairs noise than any price tier, and lubrication roughly doubles the machine's life at any price. Both are worth more than the last $300 of treadmill and both are routinely forgotten until something goes wrong.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

